1. Streams and braces — four atoms, formally paired

All four stay as independent atomic glyphs (keeping IDs 04, 05, 06, 07 intact in the register), but the register gains a pair-rule linking each to its opposite. A simple way to formalize it:

PAIR RULE (register G, provisional)
R1: ⇦ ⇄ ⇨   (white stream, left ↔ right)
R2: ❴ ⇄ ❵   (black brace, open ↔ close)

Notation: ⇦ has ⇨ as its obligate counterpart, and vice versa.
Neither member is "primary" — the pair is the unit of well-formedness.

The practical consequence: any expression (not single glyph) that opens with ❴ must eventually close with ❵ — the pair constrains syntax, but the glyphs remain individually countable, printable, and assignable to other registers (⇦/⇨ could simultaneously serve in register D as directional operators without breaking R1).

